<section class="nc-guide hub-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Planning materials for a home project</h2>
    <p>Most material shortfalls come from 3 things: measuring the plan instead of the space, forgetting waste, and buying in the wrong unit. The calculators above handle the math; this page covers the judgment around it.</p>

    <h2>Measure the space you will actually cover</h2>
    <p>Measure after demolition and framing, not from a floor plan. Rooms are rarely square, so measure each wall at both ends and use the longer number. For irregular floors, split the room into rectangles, run each one, and add the results. Closets, alcoves, and the area under appliances you plan to move are the parts people forget.</p>

    <h2>Waste allowances that hold up</h2>
    <p>Every material loses some quantity to cuts, breakage, and mistakes. These are common starting allowances; raise them for complicated layouts or a first project.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th class="num">Typical allowance</th><th>Raise it when</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="../../calculators/flooring.html">Flooring</a>, straight layout</td><td class="num">5–10%</td><td>The room has many corners, doorways, or closets</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Flooring, diagonal or herringbone</td><td class="num">15%</td><td>Planks are long or the pattern must match</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="../../calculators/tile.html">Tile</a>, straight layout</td><td class="num">10%</td><td>Tile is large format or brittle natural stone</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tile, diagonal or patterned</td><td class="num">15%</td><td>Many cuts are needed around fixtures</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="../../calculators/concrete.html">Concrete</a></td><td class="num">5–10%</td><td>The subgrade is uneven or the slab has thickened edges</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="../../calculators/paint.html">Paint</a></td><td class="num">Next full quart</td><td>Walls are textured, patched, or changing color dramatically</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Buy in the units the store sells</h2>
    <p>Calculators give exact quantities, but stores sell paint by the quart and gallon, flooring and tile by the box, and concrete by the bag or the quarter yard. Always round up to whole selling units, and compare the cost of a small extra unit (a quart, a box) with a large one (a gallon, a pallet). A 12 × 14 ft room, for example, needs 2.09 gallons of paint for 2 coats: 2 gallons plus 1 quart covers it.</p>

    <h2>Buy everything at once, and keep a little</h2>
    <p>Tile, flooring, and tinted paint can vary between production lots. Buying the full quantity at once keeps color and size consistent. Keep a few leftover tiles or planks and a small can of paint, labeled with the room and date, for repairs. A matching replacement can be hard to find a few years later.</p>
  </div>
</section>
